{"id":"42aeaf9a-9fc3-4d13-bfa3-854abeb0ddb2","object":"chat.completion","created":1785011586,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    text = case['text']\\n    passes = case['passes']\\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\\n    applications = []\\n    fixed_point = False\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        changed = False\\n        for p in passes:\\n            new_text = text.replace(p['old'], p['new'])\\n            if new_text != text:\\n                applications.append(p['name'])\\n                text = new_text\\n                changed = True\\n        if not changed:\\n            fixed_point = True\\n            break\\n    return {\\\"applications\\\": applications, \\\"fixed_point\\\": fixed_point, \\\"text\\\": text}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the problem. The task is to implement solve(case) for SE-CR-01. The input case is a payload object from the fixture. The payload has fields: text, passes (list of objects with name, old, new), max_rounds, and _case_nonce. The goal is to apply the passes repeatedly to the text, up to max_rounds, like a string rewriting system. The output should be an object with \"applications\": list of strings (the names of passes applied in order), \"fixed_point\": boolean (true if after max_rounds or when no pass applies, the text no longer changes), and \"text\": the final string.\n\nThe problem is reminiscent of \"effectslice\" tasks. The specific task is SE-CR-01. The fixture shows payloads with passes that replace substrings. For each round, we apply all passes? Or sequentially? Usually in such tasks, we apply passes in order, each pass replacing all occurrences of old with new in the current text, then proceed to next pass, then repeat the whole sequence for next round until max_rounds or no change. However, need to infer from the fixture. The passes have names like halve-a, drop-b, drop-c. In the example, initial text \"aaaa bb cc\". After applying passes:\n- halve-a: replace \"aa\" with \"a\" -> \"aa bb cc\" (since two \"aa\" become \"a a\"? Actually \"aaaa\" contains two overlapping \"aa\"? Typically replace all non-overlapping occurrences from left to right. In Python string replace, it's left to right, non-overlapping. So \"aaaa\".replace(\"aa\",\"a\") -> \"aa\" (first two become 'a', then remaining two become 'a'? Actually \"aaaa\" has positions: 0-1 'aa' replaced with 'a', then continue from position 2: \"aa\" at positions 2-3 replaced with 'a', result \"aa\". So after halve-a, \"aaaa\" -> \"aa\". So \"aaaa bb cc\" becomes \"aa bb cc\". Then drop-b: replace \"bb\" with \"b\" -> \"aa b cc\". Then drop-c: replace \" cc\" with \"\" -> \"aa b\". So after one round, \"aa b\". Then next round: halve-a: \"aa\" -> \"a\", so \"a b\". Then drop-b: \"b\"? \"bb\" not present, so unchanged. drop-c: \" cc\" not present. So after second round: \"a b\". Then third round: halve-a: \"aa\" not present, so unchanged. So fixed point reached after 2 rounds. The max_rounds is 8, so we stop after 2 rounds. Output: applications list should contain the names of passes applied in order? But careful: The task might require recording each time a pass is applied? Or the sequence of passes applied in total? The fixture output schema expects \"applications\" as array of strings. Could be the names of passes that were applied during the process (maybe only those that actually caused a change). Or maybe the sequence of passes in order across rounds. Since it's a common task in effectslice: \"applications\" is the list of rule names that were applied in order, each time a rule fires (i.e., changes the text). In many SE-CR tasks, they apply rules sequentially, and record the name of each rule that actually made a change. Then after max_rounds or until no rule fires in a whole round, stop. The \"fixed_point\" indicates whether the process ended because no rule fired in the last round (i.e., fixed point reached before max_rounds). If we hit max_rounds, fixed_point should be false? Actually if we stop because max_rounds reached, it might be that we haven't necessarily reached a fixed point, so fixed_point should be false. If we stop because no rule fired in a complete pass, then fixed_point true. Let's check typical pattern.\n\nGiven the fixture has three cases all identical. That's just for testing. We need to implement.\n\nProcedure: \n- Initialize text = case['text']\n- applications = []\n- rounds = 0\n- while rounds < case['max_rounds']:\n    any_change = False\n    for pass_ in case['passes']:\n        new_text = text.replace(pass_['old'], pass_['new'])\n        if new_text != text:\n            applications.append(pass_['name'])\n            text = new_text\n            any_change = True\n    if not any_change:\n        break\n    rounds += 1\n- After loop, fixed_point = not any_change (i.e., if we broke because no change, fixed_point True; if because max_rounds reached, fixed_point False)\n- Return {\"applications\": applications, \"fixed_point\": fixed_point, \"text\": text}\n\nBut need to ensure correct ordering: Are passes applied in the order given? Yes, fixture passes are ordered. In the example, after first round text becomes \"aa b\". That matches. Applications would be: round1: halve-a (applied), drop-b (applied), drop-c (applied). Round2: halve-a (applied), drop-b (not applied), drop-c (not applied). So applications = ['halve-a','drop-b','drop-c','halve-a']. Then fixed_point = True because after round2, no change in round3? Actually after round2, we check if any change occurred in that round; yes, halve-a changed, so we continue to next round? Wait loop: In each round, we iterate passes, record changes. After processing all passes in a round, we check if any_change was True for that round. If it was True, we continue to next round. If it was False, break. In the example, round2: halve-a changes, so any_change=True. So after round2, we increment rounds to 2? Actually rounds start at 0, after first round incremented to 1, after second round incremented to 2. Then we check while rounds < max_rounds (8), so we continue to round3. In round3, no pass changes anything, so any_change=False, then break. So fixed_point=True. Applications as above. That seems plausible.\n\nBut what about the case where max_rounds is reached before fixed point? Then fixed_point=False.\n\nNow, need to implement solve(case) returning that dict. Ensure no mutation of case. Use standard library only.\n\nEdge cases: passes may have same name? Not important.\n\nNow, output format: Must be a JSON object with single key \"implementation\", value is Python source code as a string. The source code defines solve(case). Must be properly escaped.\n\nI'll produce the code accordingly.\n\nBut also note: The task says \"dynamicKeysFrom means the object keys are arbitrary JSON strings that must be copied verbatim from the indicated input location; never parse, coerce, numerically validate, or impose a naming pattern on those keys.\" That is not relevant here because we are not outputting such keys. The output schema has fixed keys.\n\nImplementation should be straightforward.\n\nLet me write the Python function.\n\nWe need to encode as JSON string. I'll produce the implementation.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The passes list may have objects with keys like '_case_nonce' but it's not used. Only 'passes' list.\n\nAlso, note that the text can be any string, and replacement is exact substring.\n\nI'll write:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case['text']\n    passes = case['passes']\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\n    applications = []\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\n        any_change = False\n        for p in passes:\n            new_text = text.replace(p['old'], p['new'])\n            if new_text != text:\n                applications.append(p['name'])\n                text = new_text\n                any_change = True\n        if not any_change:\n            break\n    # If loop exited because break (no change), then fixed_point True; if because max_rounds reached, fixed_point False.\n    # Actually we break only when any_change is False. So after break, we know fixed_point True.\n    # If loop completed all max_rounds without break, then fixed_point False.\n    # We can set fixed_point based on whether we broke early.\n    # Use a flag or check if for loop ran to completion.\n    # Simpler: after loop, we know if we broke or not.\n    # Use else clause for for loop? But we have for _ in range(max_rounds):... else: executes if no break. So:\n    fixed_point = True  # assume break\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\n        any_change = False\n        for p in passes:\n            new_text = text.replace(p['old'], p['new'])\n            if new_text != text:\n                applications.append(p['name'])\n                text = new_text\n                any_change = True\n        if not any_change:\n            break\n    else:\n        # loop exhausted without break\n        fixed_point = False\n    # Actually need to set fixed_point correctly: if break occurred, fixed_point True; else False.\n    # Alternative: after loop, check if we broke by using a flag.\n    # Simpler: \n    # text = case['text']\n    # applications = []\n    # fixed_point = False\n    # for round_idx in range(max_rounds):\n    #    change_in_round = False\n    #    for p in passes:\n    #        new_text = text.replace(p['old'],p['new'])\n    #        if new_text != text:\n    #            applications.append(p['name'])\n    #            text = new_text\n    #            change_in_round = True\n    #    if not change_in_round:\n    #        fixed_point = True\n    #        break\n    # else:\n    #    fixed_point = False (loop completed naturally)\n    # But if break, fixed_point True. So after break, fixed_point True. If loop ends naturally, fixed_point stays False.\n    # So implement accordingly.\n\nBetter to implement with a flag.\n\nWrite:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case['text']\n    passes = case['passes']\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\n    applications = []\n    fixed_point = False\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\n        changed = False\n        for p in passes:\n            new_text = text.replace(p['old'], p['new'])\n            if new_text != text:\n                applications.append(p['name'])\n                text = new_text\n                changed = True\n        if not changed:\n            fixed_point = True\n            break\n    return {\"applications\": applications, \"fixed_point\": fixed_point, \"text\": text}\n\nThat's correct.\n\nNow, encode as JSON string. The implementation string must be a valid Python source code. I'll minify? But need to be careful with indentation and newlines. The JSON string will contain escaped newlines and quotes.\n\nI'll write the code with proper indentation and then escape for JSON.\n\nBut the instruction says: \"Encode the Python source as a JSON string, escaping newlines, backslashes, and quotes as required by JSON.\" So I need to produce a JSON object with one key \"implementation\" whose value is a string containing the Python source code. The string must be properly escaped. For example, newlines become \\n, backslashes become \\\\, quotes become \\\".\n\nI'll produce the full JSON.\n\nI'll write the source code as a single line? But it's easier to read with indentation, but escaping each newline is fine. I'll create a multi-line string in Python and then use json.dumps to get the escaped string. Since I'm outputting text, I'll manually escape.\n\nLet me write the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case['text']\n    passes = case['passes']\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\n    applications = []\n    fixed_point = False\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\n        changed = False\n        for p in passes:\n            new_text = text.replace(p['old'], p['new'])\n            if new_text != text:\n                applications.append(p['name'])\n                text = new_text\n                changed = True\n        if not changed:\n            fixed_point = True\n            break\n    return {\"applications\": applications, \"fixed_point\": fixed_point, \"text\": text}\n\nNow, I need to produce a JSON object: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    text = case['text']\\n    ...\"}.\n\nI'll construct the JSON string manually with escaped characters.\n\nTo avoid mistakes, I'll simulate by writing the Python code and then JSON-encoding it in my mind. But I can provide the final JSON.\n\nI'll do it stepwise.\n\nImplementation string (raw):\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case['text']\n    passes = case['passes']\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\n    applications = []\n    fixed_point = False\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\n        changed = False\n        for p in passes:\n            new_text = text.replace(p['old'], p['new'])\n            if new_text != text:\n                applications.append(p['name'])\n                text = new_text\n                changed = True\n        if not changed:\n            fixed_point = True\n            break\n    return {\"applications\": applications, \"fixed_point\": fixed_point, \"text\": text}\n\nNow, escape for JSON:\n- backslashes: escape as \\\\\n- double quotes: escape as \\\"\n- newlines: escape as \\n\n\nIn the raw string, there are single quotes for dict keys, but those are fine in JSON string as they are not special. However, double quotes would be problematic. In the raw string, there are no double quotes except for the keys in the return dict? Actually return dict uses double quotes: {\"applications\": ...}. Those need to be escaped as \\\". Also, the backslashes in p['new']? No, no backslashes in the code. But we have text.replace(p['old'], p['new']) - no backslashes. So only double quotes need escaping.\n\nBut careful: The source code also uses single quotes for dict keys like case['text']. That's fine. The double quotes ap