the double quote character to appear. So we write \\\". But the backslash itself is a JSON escape character, so it will be interpreted as an escape for the double quote. That's correct: the resulting string will contain a double quote character. But if the Python source code contains a backslash (e.g., for escaping a quote), we would need to double the backslash. However, in our code, we have no such backslashes. The only backslashes we are introducing are the JSON escape ones. So we need to ensure that when we write the JSON, the backslash before the double quote is not itself escaped. In JSON, to represent a double quote inside a string, we write \\\". So in the JSON text, we literally have backslash followed by double quote. That is correct. So for the return line, we write: return {\\\"applications\\\": ...}. That within the JSON string becomes: return {\\\\\"applications\\\\\":...}? No, let's step carefully.\n\nWe are constructing a JSON string that contains the Python source code. The Python source code has a line:\n    return {\"applications\": applications, \"fixed_point\": fixed_point, \"text\": text}\n\nIn that line, there are double quotes. To include that line inside a JSON string, we need to escape those double quotes. In JSON, we escape a double quote by preceding it with a backslash. So in the JSON string, we write: return {\\\"applications\\\": ...}. That means the literal characters in the JSON string are: r, e, t, u, r, n, space, {, \\, \", a, p, p, l, i, c, a, t, i, o, n, s, \\, \", :, space, a, p, p, l, i, c, a, t, i, o, n, s, ... and so on. So when the JSON parser reads this string, it will interpret the \\\" as a double quote character, resulting in the Python source code containing double quotes. That's correct. So we don't need to double the backslash. But note: The JSON string itself is enclosed in double quotes. So inside, we have to escape any double quote that is not the string delimiter. So we use \\\". That's the standard. So our final 